---
// the react implementation: what the subsite holds, and the setup. the setup
// itself is one file, stories/react/setup.md, which the react subsite's
// "getting started" renders too.
import Doc from '../../layouts/Doc.astro';
import Term from '../../components/Term.astro';
import { Content as Setup, getHeadings } from '../../../../stories/react/setup.md';
import { url, REACT, reactDocs } from '../../data/site';

const headings = [
  { depth: 2, slug: 'the-react-subsite', text: 'the react subsite' },
  ...getHeadings(),
  { depth: 2, slug: 'typescript', text: 'typescript' },
  { depth: 2, slug: 'in-a-portal-shell', text: 'in a portal shell' },
  { depth: 2, slug: 'specs', text: 'specs' },
];
---
<Doc
  frontmatter={{
    title: 'react',
    description: 'The React implementation of the nalet design system: what the React subsite holds, how to install it, what goes next to it, the stylesheet and the fonts.',
    react: 'react-getting-started',
  }}
  headings={headings}
>
  <h1>react</h1>
  <p>
    The components are written in <Term id="react">React</Term>, each with its own styles next to it, and they work with
    React 18 and 19. This site is the design guidance; the <a href={REACT}>React subsite</a> is the implementation.
  </p>

  <h2 id="the-react-subsite">the react subsite</h2>
  <p>The React subsite is built with Storybook, and lives at <a href={REACT}>/design-system/react/</a>. It holds:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>A page per component</strong>, with a link back to its guidance here. It has the
      <Term id="playground">playground</Term>, where every setting can be tried live; the examples, each with its code;
      notes on how the component is built, such as the browser element it uses, the attributes it sets and its keyboard
      map; and the table of its settings, its <Term id="prop">props</Term>.
    </li>
    <li><strong>The brand's marks</strong>: the chevron, the lockup and the icon component, with their props.</li>
    <li>
      <strong>The patterns' code</strong>: the worklist and the portal page layouts with their props, and the full
      examples of <a href={reactDocs('patterns-selection-and-bulk-actions')}>selection and bulk actions</a> and{' '}
      <a href={reactDocs('patterns-forms-and-settings')}>forms and settings</a>.
    </li>
    <li><strong>Getting started</strong>, which is the setup below, and the changelog.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    The examples on this site and on the React subsite are written once, as stories in the repository. The React subsite
    shows them with their code; this site renders the same stories in its own pages.
  </p>

  <Setup />

  <h2 id="typescript">typescript</h2>
  <p>
    The package comes with its type definitions, so your editor knows every component's props. Each component exports the
    type of its props next to it, and a helper checks a status table against the values you list, so none is forgotten.
    The typed tokens are on <a href={url('develop/tokens/')}>tokens</a>.
  </p>

  <h2 id="in-a-portal-shell">in a portal shell</h2>
  <p>
    An app that runs inside the portal shell, as a <Term id="federated-module">federated module</Term>, takes the design
    system from the shell, so a page never carries two versions of it. See
    <a href={url('develop/apps-in-a-shell/')}>apps in a shell</a>.
  </p>

  <h2 id="specs">specs</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>The brand's marks:</strong> <code>Chevron</code>, <code>Lockup</code> and <code>Icon</code>.</li>
    <li><strong>The page layouts:</strong> <code>Worklist</code> and <code>Portal</code>.</li>
    <li>
      <strong>The examples:</strong> written once in <code>stories/**/*.stories.tsx</code>; this site renders them through
      Storybook's portable stories.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Types:</strong> each component's props type next to it (<code>ButtonProps</code>, <code>DataTableColumn</code>,
      <code>ObjectState</code> …); <code>defineStates</code> checks a status table against the keys you list.
    </li>
  </ul>
</Doc>
